[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
DNS on SRV1 is up and authoritative for the campus zone; there is no outage to fix. From three different client segments (pc1, pc2, pc3), queries to 10.5.0.4 resolve internal names correctly (e.g., srv1.jeremysitlab.com -> 10.5.0.4), and SRV1 responds authoritatively to zone SOA queries. SRV1 is reachable (ICMP from all PCs succeeds), and the named daemon is running and listening on 10.5.0.4:53/udp and tcp (ps shows “named -c /etc/bind/named.conf”; ss/netstat show port 53 listeners). The zone file /etc/bind/db.jeremysitlab includes an NS, A for srv1, and a wildcard A, so any host under jeremysitlab.com should resolve.

What users likely observed is that SRV1 is configured as an authoritative-only DNS (recursion no), so lookups for non-campus names (e.g., internet hosts) are refused; dig warns “recursion requested but not available.” If users tried external sites or relied on a search suffix for bare hostnames, they would perceive “DNS is down” even though internal jeremysitlab.com resolution works.

Localization: SRV1 (10.5.0.4), BIND named process. Note: systemctl shows bind9.service inactive (dead), but named is running directly; this does not affect service availability.

Root cause: Misinterpretation—SRV1 is authoritative-only by design; clients expecting recursive resolution (or lacking a search domain) saw failures unrelated to SRV1 availability.

Corrective fix: No restart needed. If recursive/internet resolution is desired, enable forwarders/recursion in /etc/bind/named.conf.options and reload named; optionally enable/manage via systemctl for monitoring, and ensure DHCP provides the jeremysitlab.com search domain. 
=== END ===